Sign Poly1305's function pointers on arm64e

poly1305_init stores the addresses of poly1305_blocks and poly1305_emit (or
their NEON variants) in ctx->func, and poly1305.c calls them through C function
pointers. On arm64e every indirect call authenticates the pointer with the IA
key and a zero discriminator, so the unsigned addresses crash on first use.
This breaks every Poly1305 user, including ChaCha20-Poly1305.

paciza signs them the way the compiler signs C function pointers. No other
aarch64 assembly in OpenSSL 3.5.9 stores code addresses for C to call.
Found and tested for Shello (Vendor/test-openssl-arm64e.sh).

--- a/crypto/poly1305/asm/poly1305-armv8.pl
+++ b/crypto/poly1305/asm/poly1305-armv8.pl
@@ -108,6 +108,14 @@
 
 	csel	$d0,$d0,$r0,eq
 	csel	$d1,$d1,$r1,eq
+
+#ifdef	__arm64e__
+	// poly1305.c calls these through C function pointers, which arm64e
+	// authenticates with the IA key and a zero discriminator. Sign them
+	// the same way, or the first call crashes.
+	paciza	$d0
+	paciza	$d1
+#endif
 
 #ifdef	__ILP32__
 	stp	w12,w13,[$len]
